## What Happens When a Contract Breaks

### requires Violation
```xiom
fn withdraw(balance: &mut Int, amount: Int)
  requires: amount > 0;
  requires: balance >= amount;
{ balance = balance - amount; }

fn main() -> Int {
  var b = 50;
  withdraw(&mut b, 100);  // VIOLATION!
  return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
CONTRACT VIOLATION: withdraw
  requires: balance >= amount
  at lesson.xi:11 (called from main at lesson.xi:17)
  values: balance = 50, amount = 100
```

### ensures Violation
```xiom
fn absolute(x: Int) -> Int
  ensures: result >= 0;
{
  if x < 0 { return x; }  // BUG: should return -x
  return x;
}
```
Output:
```
CONTRACT VIOLATION: absolute
  ensures: result >= 0
  at lesson.xi:5
  values: result = -5
```

### The Trap Mechanism
The program calls @llvm.trap which prints diagnostics and terminates. This is intentional — failing fast prevents corrupted state from propagating.

